    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change adds a `telephoneDisplay` field to the owner response: the stored E.164 `telephone` formatted for humans as *country code \u00b7 space \u00b7 national digits grouped in threes* (e.g. `+61412345678` \u2192 `+61 412 345 678`), while `telephone` itself stays raw E.164.\n\nThree edits, matching the existing derived-field pattern:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `telephoneDisplay` string property to the `Owner` schema (alongside `displayName`/`initials`), so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field.\n\n2. **`util/TelephoneDisplay.java`** (new) \u2014 a small formatter mirroring the existing util classes (`CheckDigit`, `MembershipLevel`, \u2026). It recognizes the country code from the same known prefixes as the inbound `TelephoneE164` normalizer (`61`, `1`; longest-first), then groups the remaining national digits in threes. Null-safe; falls back to plain three-digit grouping for an unrecognized/malformed number so it never throws on seed data.\n\n3. **`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 imported the util and added `@Mapping(target = \"telephoneDisplay\", expression = \"java(TelephoneDisplay.format(owner.getTelephone()))\")`.\n\n`./mvnw -o test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` (`coreFormatsTelephoneDisplay` asserts both `$.telephone` = `+61412345678` and `$.telephoneDisplay` = `+61 412 345 678`). That acceptance test is the only test class in the project, so the whole suite passes.",
